a sentence holding [partial sum(s)] and [exact] or [exactly] is a claim: true
its nearest neighbours are not: a negation, another word, a sentence apart: true
a known gap, pinned: bare [partials] is not the phrase: true
a decimal point ends no sentence: the phrase and [exact] around 0.5 are one claim: true
the claim is reported whitespace-collapsed, one per sentence: true
a comment holding a string with a comment's closing delimiter is one comment: true
a character literal of a double quote opens no string: true
a nested comment is read with its parent: true
a string literal is read decoded: an escape spelling [exact] is the word: true
code is not prose: an identifier spelling the words is not a claim: true
a source that does not lex is refused as the input's fault, with its line: true
and so is one that lexes but does not parse: true
a comment inside the item that derives the premise is covered: true
a comment leading the item that derives it is covered: true
a claim label in the deriving item is covered: true
a file header ahead of an unrelated item is uncovered, though a later item derives: true
a derivation named only in prose covers nothing: true
a bare exact_partials after an open is not the path, so it covers nothing: true
nor does a module alias's: true
a trailing comment after the last item has no item to derive it: true
the reader counts the items that derive: true
an uncovered sentence in a file without a row is a finding, one per sentence: true
a row holding the file's count exactly absorbs it: true
a row holding another count is a finding: a new sentence, or a conversion the row missed: true
an Every row absorbs any count: true
a row whose file holds no uncovered sentence is stale, and so is a row naming no source: true
an earned row is not stale: true
two rows naming one file are refused: true
